{
 "id": "2024-wang-classroom",
 "name": "Classroom Design for Concentration and Creativity",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: 19 rendered views of the same university classroom (48 seats). Sixteen combine four design elements in two levels each, space shape (curvilinear / rectilinear walls, ceiling lights and table corners), furniture arrangement (collaborative clusters / traditional front-facing rows), ceiling height (4 m / 2.7 m) and wall colour (deep blue 8.7B 4/5 / deep red 2.5R 4/8); three more show a rectilinear traditional low room with white walls (A17), with a full glass wall onto trees (A18) and a curvilinear collaborative high blue room without windows (A19). As in the paper, participants first see a grey screen for 15 s and then each classroom for 15 s with a 15 s grey screen between them (about 10 minutes, in random order). Afterwards they choose, from all 19 classrooms shown side by side, the one where they feel they could concentrate best and the one where they could be most creative, and answer age, gender and whether they study a housing or interior design-related field. Rebuilt from Wang, Cho & Hong (2024), Brain and Subjective Responses to Indoor Environments Related to Concentration and Creativity, Sensors 24(23): 7838, with the images taken from its Table 2 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids follow the paper's codes A1-A19 (see its Table 2: A1 = curvilinear, traditional, low, red; A4 = curvilinear, collaborative, high, blue; A17 white walls; A18 glass wall with nature view). Analysis as in the paper: frequency of each classroom chosen for concentration and for creativity, compared by design element; the paper (20 students and staff) found the white-walled traditional classroom A17 chosen most for concentration (45%, then A12 20%) and the nature-view classroom A18 most for creativity (45%, then A4 25%), with front-facing rows linked to concentration and curvilinear, collaborative rooms to creativity. Differences from the original study: (1) online, without the EEG recording (concentration index and relative theta), the 30 s eyes-closed rest and the on-screen guides; (2) the paper used two fixed presentation orders (sets A and B) that it does not print, so the order is random here; (3) the paper does not print the post-survey questions, so the wording of the two choice questions and of the age, gender and field-of-study questions is ours; (4) the images are the copies printed in Table 2 (about 420 px wide), not the full renders; (5) the paper's participants were 20 students and staff at one Korean university aged 18-29; anyone can take part here.",
